{
  "name": "regex-ignore-case-ascii-fold",
  "description": "Case-insensitive matching (`i`) follows Dart's RegExp (ECMAScript) for the four letters that Unicode folds into ASCII: the long s (U+017F), the Kelvin sign (U+212A), the dotless i (U+0131) and the dotted capital I (U+0130). With `gi` (no `u`) none of them matches an ASCII letter, because the non-Unicode canonicalization never maps a non-ASCII character to an ASCII one: `[a-z]` on \"\u017f\u212a\u0131\u0130sk\" gives \"\u017f\u212a\u0131\u0130##\", `s` on \"\u017fS\" gives \"\u017f#\", `k` on \"\u212ak\" gives \"\u212a#\", `\\w` on \"\u017f\u212ax\" gives \"\u017f\u212a#\" and `[^a-z]` on \"\u017f\u212a\u0131\u0130a\" gives \"####a\". With `giu` simple case folding applies: U+017F is s and U+212A is k (\"##\u0131\u0130##\", \"##\", \"##\", \"###\", \"\u017f\u212a##a\"), while U+0131 and U+0130 have no simple folding and still match no ASCII letter. Guard, both modes: non-ASCII letters still fold among themselves, `\u00e9` on \"\u00c9\" gives \"#\". Every replace-mode regex puts \"#\" for each match. Every input reads page state, so the engine keeps the Values unresolved with `watches` and the client resolves them.",
  "input": {
    "type": "Column",
    "key": "col",
    "children": [
      {
        "type": "Text",
        "key": "range_i",
        "props": {
          "data": {
            "type": "transform",
            "input": {
              "type": "state",
              "scope": "page",
              "key": "mix"
            },
            "by": [
              {
                "type": "regex",
                "pattern": "[a-z]",
                "flags": "gi",
                "replacement": "#"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      },
